  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOverall, the period from Sunday night through Monday night will be\nrelatively quiet comparatively, with low shower and thunderstorm \nchances focused along and south of I-40 through Monday afternoon\nand dry conditions Monday night. Temperatures during both the\novernight and the daytime will continue to be above seasonal\nnormals.\n\nThe focus of the forecast next week remains on the much\nanticipated slow-moving storm system set to move across the\nsouthern United States and the increased likelihood of a heavy\nrainfall event for the region. The evolution of the storm system\nremains very much uncertain, but model trends point toward a\nmore delayed eastward movement than previous. After the\naforementioned dry Monday night, shower and thunderstorm chances\nbegin to increase once again across mainly northeast Oklahoma\nduring the day Tuesday and into Tuesday night in response to a \nlead disturbance forecast to move through the region ahead of the \nmain upper level system. This should keep high temperatures cooler\nacross northern areas, with summer-like temperatures hanging on \nsouth of I-40. Tuesday nights lows will be warmer than previous\nnights, reflecting an increase low level wind speeds and moisture.\nAs the main storm system shifts east toward mid to late week, \nhigh shower and thunderstorm chances will spread from west to\neast, with multiple rounds of heavy rainfall possible given\nprecipitable water forecasts near seasonal maxes. Temperatures\nduring the latter part of next week and into the early part of the\nnext weekend should be below normal and more fall-like. Current\nprojections indicate that the main storm system should shift east\nof the area by the weekend, with only low chances for additional\nrainfall lingering.",
